django.template.base.VariableDoesNotExist: Failed lookup for key [errors] in [{'True': True, 'False': False, 'None': None}, {'widget': {'name': 'body', 'is_hidden': False, 'required': False, 'value': '<p>For decades during the 1900s, Finnish athletes trained for winter cross-country skiing by walking in the summer with ski poles — a practice they called <i>“ski-walking.”</i> This simple off-season training method later evolved into what we now know as Nordic Walking.</p><p>In 1966, <strong>Leena Jääskeläinen</strong> (born 1939), a gym teacher in Viherlaakso, Helsinki, Finland, began using “walking with ski poles” in physical education sessions for her students. Later, as a faculty member of the Physical Education Department at the University of Jyväskylä, she continued to promote this form of exercise. In 1987, she presented her ideas on exercise walking with poles publicly for the first time at the <i>Finlandia Walk (“Finlandia kävely”)</i> in Tampere, Finland. Her work caught the attention of the Finnish Central Association for Recreational Sports and Outdoor Activities, helping to popularize the concept nationally.</p><p>From Finland, Nordic Walking quickly spread to <strong>Sweden, Denmark, and throughout Europe</strong>, then to the <strong>USA</strong> and around the world. Today, it’s estimated that over <strong>12 million Europeans</strong> practice Nordic Walking. The Helsinki-based <strong>International Nordic Walking Federation</strong> (<a href="https://inwa-nordicwalking.com" target="_blank">inwa-nordicwalking.com</a>) has conducted extensive research showing Nordic Walking’s positive health effects for people with arthritis, heart disease, diabetes, and other conditions.</p><h3>What’s the Difference Between Nordic Walking and Ski Poles?</h3><p>Although both types of poles assist in movement, they are designed for very different purposes:</p><ul><li><strong>Ski Poles:</strong> Intended for snow-based activities such as cross-country or downhill skiing. They are designed for propulsion on snow and vary depending on the skiing style.</li><li><strong>Nordic Walking Poles:</strong> Specifically made for walking and hiking on various terrains — grass, dirt, sand, asphalt, or snow-free trails. They are shorter than ski poles and often come in <i>one-piece</i> or <i>adjustable</i> designs.</li><li><strong>Materials:</strong> Common materials include <i>aluminum, carbon fiber, or fiberglass</i> for a lightweight yet durable build.</li><li><strong>Support and Stability:</strong> Nordic Walking poles feature adjustable lengths, <i>rubber tips</i> for pavement, and <i>carbide tips</i> for off-road grip.</li><li><strong>Grips:</strong> Ergonomically designed for walking, Nordic Walking grips are often cushioned and shaped for comfort and efficiency. They allow for a relaxed hand motion and effective use of straps.</li></ul><p>In short, Nordic Walking poles are optimized for balance, comfort, and versatility across different terrains, while ski poles are specialized tools for snow-based propulsion and stability.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>', 'attrs': {'class': 'django_ckeditor_5', 'id': 'id_body'}, 'template_name': 'django_ckeditor_5/widget.html'}, 'config': {'toolbar': ['heading', 'codeBlock', '|', 'outdent', 'indent', '|', 'bold', 'italic', 'link', 'underline', 'strikethrough', 'code', 'subscript', 'superscript', 'highlight', '|', 'bulletedList', 'numberedList', 'todoList', '|', 'blockQuote', 'insertImage', '|', 'fontSize', 'fontFamily', 'fontColor', 'fontBackgroundColor', 'mediaEmbed', 'removeFormat', 'insertTable', 'sourceEditing'], 'language': 'en', 'blockToolbar': ['paragraph', 'heading1', 'heading2', 'heading3', '|', 'bulletedList', 'numberedList', '|', 'blockQuote'], 'image': {'toolbar': ['imageTextAlternative', '|', 'imageStyle:alignLeft', 'imageStyle:alignRight', 'imageStyle:alignCenter', 'imageStyle:side', '|', 'toggleImageCaption', '|'], 'styles': ['full', 'side', 'alignLeft', 'alignRight', 'alignCenter']}, 'table': {'conten